1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. Qc2 d5 5. a3 Be7 ({Usual and undoubtedly better is} 5... Bxc3+ 6. Qxc3 Ne4 {Black changes back into an ordinary queen’s gambit in which he has made me a present of the move 5 a3, because his bishop could, of course, have gone to e7 in one move only instead of two.}) 6. Nf3 Nbd7 7. Bf4 Nh5 ({Black does not capture with} 7... dxc4 {at once because of the reply} 8. e4 Nb6 9. Nb5 {But now ...dxc4 is in the air. A critical stage has been reached. In this tournament I had won two games of over seventy moves, stubborn positional struggles, avoiding all risk. But here I realised that an entirely different type of game was shaping. 8 e3 Nxf4 9 exf5 dxc4 10 Bxc4 would smash up my pawn position but leave me with four pieces in good play, against my opponent's two in bad play. By speedy development and direct attack the ad­vantage in development might be converted into a quick win, whereas anything quiet would allow Black to make up his leeway. So...}) 8. e3 Nxf4 9. exf4 dxc4 10. Bxc4 O-O 11. O-O-O c6 {A better move than it looks, blocking Black’s d5 effectively and threatening 12...b5 13 Ba2 b4, forcing open the b-file. White's reply not only takes the c3-N into the direct attack on the king but, by removing it from c3, enables ...b4 to be answered by a4, keeping the queen’s side closed.} 12. Ne4 Nf6 13. Neg5 b5 14. Ba2 Bd7 15. Ne5 Be8 16. h4 {This would always have been the answer to ...h6 - do you see why? As Black’s g-pawn can be forced forward to g6, 16 h4 offers the best way of developing White's h1-rook.} a5 17. Bb1 g6 18. h5 Qd5 19. hxg6 fxg6 {Given the respite of a casual move or two, Black, by ...a4 and ...b4, or ...c5, could himself attack White's king. Not a moment may be wasted; that is why some players are not happy in this type of position, preferring an advantage, even though smaller, of a more solid type and a less tense, whilst more laborious, struggle. But as I mentioned in a previous article, to have special predilections is to have chinks in your armour - you must try to be equally at home in every type of position. Here we find there is a combination which clinches the issue immediately.} 20. Nxh7 $1 Nxh7 21. Nxg6 Rf7 ({After} 21... Bxg6 22. Qxg6+ {, Black would be mated. 21...Rf7 is forced but allows us to recover our piece remaining two pawns up, with Black's king stripped of all pawn shelter. We are beginning to enjoy this combinative attacking play but, analysing ahead, per­ceive that it has yielded all it can. So on move 25 we offer to exchange queens and transpose into a dull end­game; the fact that this would be quite hopeless for Black, making him worsen his own queen's position to avoid it. Once again we cash-in on our readiness to accept any type of play. If we tried to finish the game by continuing the attack at all costs, we might find our task much harder.}) 22. Nxe7+ Rxe7 23. Rxh7 Raa7 24. Rxe7 Rxe7 25. Qc5 Qd7 26. Ba2 Bg6 27. f5 {Source: Illustrated London News, 6 May 1950} (27. f5 {If} Bxf5 $2 ({if} 27... Bf7 28. f6 Re8 29. Qg5+) 28. Qxf5) 1-0

